<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mukachevo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Munkács</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mukachevo, in Hungarian Munkács, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Zakarpattia</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Its hilltop Palanok Castle belonged to the Rákóczi family, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilona-zrinyi">Ilona Zrínyi</a> defended it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> from 1685 to 1688. Part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> for centuries, the city passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1920, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945 and Ukraine in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mukachevo.jpg" alt="Mukachevo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kiszo.net · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Munkacsivar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mukachevo lies in the valley of the Latorica River in Zakarpattia Oblast, close to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. It is a rail terminus and highway junction. The name most probably derives from the Hungarian surname Muncas, from munkás, worker, though another theory points to a Proto-Slavic root meaning flour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>'s parliament officially renamed the city from Mukacheve to Mukachevo in 2017, after it emerged that the former spelling, in use since 1947, had come about by a mistake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations have found a Celtic oppidum of the 3rd to 1st century BCE and an Iron Age Thracian fort in the area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled it in the 6th century, and Hungarian tribes entered the Carpathian Basin in 895. In 1397 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, granted the town to the exiled prince of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania Fyodor Koriatovych, who settled many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a> there; other sources say he bought it in 1396. It became a Hungarian royal free city in 1445 and later part of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Imre Thököly's revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> started from Munkács in 1678, and Palanok Castle was important in Ferenc II Rákóczi's war of independence.</p><p>After 1711 the city came under Austrian control. In 1726 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> gave the castle and town to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonborn-family">Schönborn family</a>, who settled many Germans there. The castle served as a prison from 1796 to 1897 and held the Greek leader Alexander Ypsilanti from 1821 to 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mukachevo became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> on 4 June 1920, was re-annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1938 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-vienna-award">First Vienna Award</a>, passed to the Soviet Ukraine in 1945 and is now in Ukraine. Jews made up about half the town's population before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and it was known for its Hasidic community under Chief Rabbi Chaim Elazar Spira, who led it from 1913 until his death in 1937. The remaining Jews, nearly 15,000 in the spring of 1944, were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>. A new synagogue was dedicated in 2006. Palanok Castle dates from the 14th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mukachevo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His marriage to Ilona Zrínyi, widow of Francis I Rákóczi, brought him the great Rákóczi estates and the castle of <b>Munkács</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
